1. Final scrutiny of the original workbook
I examined the five worksheets and their formulas.

The workbook has a good logical foundation, but I identified the following issues.
1.1 Asset registration and identification
Your Household Items Register uses XLOOKUP to retrieve an item's ID and category based on its name.

For example, the formula uses the item name to retrieve its corresponding ID from the Database worksheet.

Problem: An item name is not a sufficiently reliable unique identifier.

Consider two televisions in the same household. They may have the same name and category but must have separate records because they can occupy different locations and have different movement histories.

Correction:

Every physical item must have its own unique asset ID, irrespective of whether another item has the same name.
Asset ID	Asset name	Category
AST-00001	Television	Electronics
AST-00002	Television	Electronics
AST-00003	Refrigerator	Kitchen appliances
AST-00004	Laptop	Electronics

The ID should be generated when an asset is registered, rather than retrieved through an item-name lookup.
1.2 Item movement log
Your movement log contains:
- Date
- Time
- Item ID
- Item Name
- Previous Location
- New Location
- Moved By
This is a reasonable starting structure.

However, the current movement records contain formulas linking the item ID and name to fixed rows in the asset register.

Problem: This approach makes movement recording dependent on the original row positions in the register. It is not a robust way to identify an asset in a growing system.

There is also a sample-data inconsistency: the movement times in the workbook are not in chronological order across the displayed records.

Correction:

Every movement must be a separate transaction linked directly to the asset's unique ID.

I would also introduce:
- Movement reference number.
- Movement reason.
- Requested by.
- Approved by, where applicable.
- Movement status.
- Date and time recorded.
- Actual movement date and time, if different from the recording time.
This distinction matters because a movement may be recorded after the physical transfer has taken place.
1.3 Current Item Locations
Your Current Item Locations worksheet currently retrieves the new location, date and time from corresponding rows in the movement log.

Problem: It does not independently establish the latest confirmed movement for each asset.

An asset might have been moved five times, but the application must always display its latest confirmed location, regardless of the number of transactions or their display order.

Correction:

The system must maintain one current-location record for every registered asset.

Whenever a movement is confirmed:
1. Validate the existing location.
2. Record the movement transaction.
3. Update the asset's current location.
4. Update its last confirmed movement date and time.
All four operations must succeed together.
1.4 Movement Summary
Your existing Movement Summary worksheet displays items alongside their previous and new locations.

Problem: It is largely linked to corresponding rows in the movement log, rather than functioning as a complete movement analysis.

This means it does not adequately represent multiple movements of the same item.

Correction:

The new system should generate summaries from the entire movement history.

It should support summaries by:
- Asset.
- Category.
- Previous location.
- New location.
- Date range.
- User.
- Department.
- Movement status.
1.5 Database worksheet
Your Database worksheet currently stores item names, item IDs, categories, users and locations.

This is suitable for a small Excel demonstration, but it mixes different types of information within one worksheet.

There are also category inconsistencies in the sample records. For instance, the laptop stand and sofa are classified under categories that should be reviewed and standardised.

Correction:

The SaaS should use separate, related database tables for assets, categories, users, locations and movement transactions.

This will make the system easier to maintain and prevent inconsistent records.
1.6 Other workbook limitations
Area	Issue	Final correction
Capacity	Fixed input ranges	Dynamic database records
Categories	Inconsistent classifications	Standard category management
Locations	Fixed dropdown list	Custom location hierarchy
Date and time	Dependent on spreadsheet cells	Server-recorded timestamps
User tracking	Limited identification	Authenticated user accounts
Movement validation	Basic location comparison	Full transaction validation
Data integrity	Formula-dependent	Database constraints and transactions
History	Limited summary	Permanent transaction history

These findings concern the uploaded workbook's current implementation, not merely the general concept.

2. Final product definition
AssetTrack
ASSET MANAGEMENT AND TRACKING SYSTEM










dribbble.com


Tagline: Know what you own. Know where it is. Know who is responsible.

Product definition

AssetTrack is a centralised digital platform that enables households and organisations to register, classify, locate, allocate, monitor, maintain and audit their physical assets.

Product type: Web-based Asset Management System.

Operating model: Multi-user, multi-location and multi-organisation.

Primary interface: Responsive web application accessible through computers, tablets and smartphones.

Primary objectives
- Maintain a reliable register of physical assets.
- Provide accurate current-location information.
- Document every asset movement.
- Establish responsibility and accountability.
- Monitor asset conditions and maintenance.
- Generate accurate management and audit reports.

2.1 The scope of the product
I recommend that AssetTrack support two main account environments:

A. Household environment

For individuals and families who want to monitor furniture, electronics, appliances and other possessions.

B. Organisation environment

For schools, offices, hotels, churches, businesses and institutions that need more advanced controls.

Both environments should use the same underlying asset-management engine, but the organisation environment should have departments, staff accounts, approvals and more detailed reporting.

This avoids developing two separate applications.
2.2 What AssetTrack is not
To avoid unnecessary complexity, the product should not initially be presented as:
- A GPS tracking application.
- A procurement management system.
- An accounting application.
- A warehouse logistics platform.
- A financial subscription-management application.
- An enterprise resource planning system.
It may integrate with some of these systems in the future, but they are not its primary purpose.

The application tracks recorded asset locations. QR-code scanning does not independently establish an item's physical location.

3. Final functional modules
I recommend retaining ten functional modules, but refining their boundaries to avoid duplicated functions.
Module 1: Asset Registration and Asset Register







mobiess.com


This is the foundation of the entire application.

Required asset information
Field	Requirement
Asset ID	System-generated and unique
Asset Name	Mandatory
Category	Mandatory
Subcategory	Optional
Description	Optional
Brand	Optional
Model	Optional
Serial Number	Optional
Acquisition Date	Optional
Acquisition Cost	Optional
Initial Location	Mandatory
Current Location	Initially equals initial location
Responsible Person	Optional for household assets; configurable for organisations
Department	Optional
Condition	Mandatory
Asset Photograph	Optional
QR Code	System-generated

Required operations
- Add an asset.
- Edit asset details.
- View asset information.
- Search for an asset.
- Filter assets.
- Upload photographs.
- Import assets from Excel.
- Export the asset register.
- Archive assets.
Important correction: Asset ID, asset name and serial number must not be treated as interchangeable.

The asset ID is the system's permanent internal identifier. The serial number is a manufacturer-provided identifier and may be absent.

An archived asset must retain its historical information.
Module 2: Asset Movement Management







itemit.com


This is the central operational module.

Required movement information
Field	Requirement
Movement ID	Automatically generated
Asset ID	Mandatory
Previous Location	Retrieved automatically
New Location	Selected by user
Movement Date	Mandatory
Movement Time	Mandatory
Movement Reason	Mandatory
Requested By	Automatically identified
Approved By	If approval is required
Remarks	Optional
Status	System-controlled

Movement reasons should include:
- Internal transfer.
- Change of assigned department.
- Temporary relocation.
- Maintenance.
- Repair.
- Return to original location.
- Other.
Movement statuses:
- Draft.
- Pending approval.
- Approved.
- Completed.
- Rejected.
- Cancelled.
A key distinction is necessary here:

An approved movement is not necessarily a completed movement. Where physical confirmation is required, the system must record completion separately.

