The system must prevent a second movement from being completed against a stale previous location.

For example, if two staff members independently attempt to move the same asset from Room A, but the first movement has already moved it to Room B, the second transaction must be rejected or reviewed.
Module 3: Current Asset Locations







appsmith.com


This module should display the latest confirmed location of every asset.

Required fields
- Asset ID.
- Asset name.
- Category.
- Current location.
- Responsible person.
- Condition.
- Asset status.
- Last confirmed movement date.
- Last confirmed movement time.
Important design decision

Current location and responsible person must be separate fields.

A laptop can be physically located in the chemistry laboratory but assigned to a staff member who is responsible for it.

The system must not assume that the person responsible for an asset is necessarily the person who moved it.
Module 4: Movement History and Audit Trail
This module contains two related but distinct records.

Movement history: Records the physical movement of assets.

System audit trail: Records important actions performed by users within the application.

For example:
Event	Movement history	Audit trail
Asset moved	Yes	Yes
Asset registered	No	Yes
Asset information edited	No	Yes
Movement approved	Yes, as part of transaction	Yes
Asset archived	No	Yes
User permission changed	No	Yes

This distinction is important. A system audit log should not be confused with the physical movement history.

Historical movement records should not be directly deleted or overwritten. Corrections should create traceable corrective transactions.
Module 5: Asset Condition and Maintenance







andami.co.kr


I recommend separating two concepts:

Asset condition describes the physical state of the item.
- Excellent.
- Good.
- Fair.
- Damaged.
- Unserviceable.
Asset operational status describes its current availability or lifecycle.
- Active.
- Under maintenance.
- Missing.
- Retired.
- Disposed.
- Archived.
An item can be physically in good condition but temporarily unavailable because it is being serviced.

Maintenance information
- Maintenance ID.
- Asset ID.
- Maintenance type.
- Problem description.
- Reported date.
- Assigned technician.
- Start date.
- Completion date.
- Repair cost.
- Work performed.
- Maintenance status.
- Supporting documents.
The system should also provide scheduled maintenance reminders.
Module 6: Asset Allocation and Responsibility
This module manages who is responsible for an asset.

It should support:
- Assigning assets to users.
- Assigning assets to departments.
- Transferring responsibility.
- Returning assigned assets.
- Recording assignment dates.
- Recording acknowledgement of receipt.
- Viewing allocation history.
The allocation history must be retained even when responsibility changes.

For example, if a school transfers a laptop from the principal to the ICT officer, the system should record both assignments and their respective dates.
Module 7: Dashboard and Reporting







assetprime.org


The dashboard should provide a quick overview of the organisation's assets.

Core dashboard indicators
Indicator	Calculation or meaning
Total Assets	Number of registered assets
Active Assets	Assets with active operational status
Missing Assets	Assets currently reported missing
Damaged Assets	Assets requiring attention
Under Maintenance	Assets with active maintenance cases
Total Categories	Number of asset categories
Total Locations	Number of registered locations
Pending Movements	Movement requests awaiting a decision
Completed Movements	Completed transactions within selected period

The dashboard should also include:
- Asset distribution by category.
- Asset distribution by location.
- Asset condition distribution.
- Movement trends over time.
- Maintenance activity.
- Missing-asset reports.
Important reporting rule: Total assets should not be calculated by adding active, missing, damaged and under-maintenance counts. Those classifications can overlap. The dashboard must use clearly defined, mutually exclusive operational statuses or label overlapping condition indicators appropriately.
Module 8: User and Access Management
The system should support these roles:
Role	Permissions
Platform Administrator	Technical and platform-level administration
Organisation Administrator	Manages organisation settings and users
Asset Manager	Manages asset records and movement operations
Department Manager	Manages departmental assets and approvals
Staff Member	Views authorised records and submits requests
Auditor	Read-only access to permitted records and reports

A household account may use a simplified version of these permissions.

Access should be configurable rather than assuming that every organisation has the same management structure.
Module 9: Notifications and Alerts
The system should generate notifications for:
- Pending movement approvals.
- Approved or rejected movements.
- Completed asset transfers.
- Missing asset reports.
- Damaged asset reports.
- Maintenance deadlines.
- Assignment and return requests.
- Important changes to asset records.
Notifications should be generated from actual system events, not simply from a user opening a particular screen.
Module 10: Location and Organisation Management
This module manages the structure in which assets are located.

For organisations, location levels could include:
School premises

Administration block

Principal's office

Accounts office


Science block

Chemistry laboratory

Biology laboratory




The location system should support:
- Creating locations.
- Editing locations.
- Deactivating unused locations.
- Assigning parent locations.
- Viewing assets within a location.
- Viewing location movement history.
A location should not be deleted if it has historical asset transactions. It should be deactivated instead.

4. Final database design
The earlier proposal identified the main tables, but I would make the structure more explicit now.

The revised system should use PostgreSQL with the following tables.
No.	Table	Purpose
1	Organisations	Stores household or organisational account information
2	Users	Stores authenticated user information
3	Organisation_Memberships	Links users to organisations
4	Roles	Defines user roles
5	Permissions	Defines permitted system actions
6	User_Roles	Links users to roles
7	Categories	Stores asset classifications
8	Assets	Stores the main asset register
9	Locations	Stores hierarchical locations
10	Departments	Stores organisational departments
11	Asset_Assignments	Records asset responsibility
12	Asset_Movements	Stores movement transactions
13	Movement_Approvals	Records approval decisions
14	Maintenance_Records	Stores maintenance information
15	Asset_Condition_History	Records condition changes
16	Attachments	Stores supporting document references
17	Notifications	Stores user notifications
18	Audit_Logs	Records important system actions

